---
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
import home from '../../assets/projects/union-website/home.png';
import homeSurfaces from '../../assets/projects/union-website/home-surfaces.png';
import membersApp from '../../assets/projects/union-website/members-app.png';
import contractors from '../../assets/projects/union-website/contractors.png';
import healthWelfare from '../../assets/projects/union-website/health-welfare.png';
import contact from '../../assets/projects/union-website/contact.png';
import signin from '../../assets/projects/union-website/signin.png';
import phoneHome from '../../assets/projects/union-website/phone-home.png';
import phoneMenu from '../../assets/projects/union-website/phone-menu.png';
import phoneHealth from '../../assets/projects/union-website/phone-health.png';

const desktopSizes = '(min-width: 1000px) 960px, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const pairSizes = '(min-width: 1000px) 470px, (min-width: 900px) 45vw, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const phoneSizes = '(min-width: 760px) 230px, (min-width: 600px) 30vw, 64vw';

const pages = [
  { name: 'Home', text: 'Who the local is, and a slide for each part of the platform behind the site.' },
  { name: 'Members', text: 'The member app, what it shows, and how members organize together.' },
  { name: 'Contractors', text: 'What the contractor portal does, and where its view of the shared record stops.' },
  { name: 'Health & Welfare', text: 'How hours worked become health coverage, and what to do after an injury on the job.' },
  { name: 'About, Our Team, and Resources', text: 'The local’s affiliations, its elected officers, and a directory of training funds, safety agencies, and voter registration in English and Spanish.' },
  { name: 'Contact and Sign in', text: 'The hall’s details with a short written-enquiry form, and the website’s own sign-in.' },
];

const phoneScreens = [
  { src: phoneHome, alt: 'The home page on a phone, with the welcome headline and the button to download the members app' },
  { src: phoneMenu, alt: 'The mobile navigation menu open, with five pages and the sign-in and contact buttons' },
  { src: phoneHealth, alt: 'The Health and Welfare page on a phone, listing the four steps from hours worked to coverage' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  name="Labor Union Website"
  description="Case study: the public website Sām Kazemi designed and built for a labor union local at Workforce Systems, one front door for its members, contractors, and community."
  summary="A nine-page public website for a union local: one front door for its members, its contractors, and the community, built on the same design system as the workforce platform behind it."
  role="Lead Developer: design and front end"
  org="Workforce Systems (WFS)"
  period="Aug 2026 – Present"
  status="Built; launch planned"
  accent="lapis"
  stack={[
    'HTML5 / CSS',
    'Tailwind CSS',
    'JavaScript',
    'Responsive design',
    'Accessible UI',
    'Design systems',
    'Information architecture',
  ]}
>
  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p>
    <h2>One front door for three audiences</h2>
    <p>
      A union local’s website is read by very different people. Members want to know where they stand
      and how to get to work. Contractors who hire through the hall want to know how to ask for a
      crew. Neighbors and future members want to know who the local is and how to reach it. Each of
      them needs an answer in a page or two, and many will be reading on a phone.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is also the public face of the{' '}
      <a href={url('/projects/workforce-systems')}>Union Workforce Ecosystem</a>, the platform
      Workforce Systems is building for the same union. Sām designed and built it as part of that
      contract, so a visitor who signs in steps from the website into the platform without feeling
      like they changed products.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={home}
        alt="The website home page: a welcome headline over a photo of a construction crew, with buttons to download the members app or learn more"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The home page. The union’s name, logo, address, phone numbers, and officers are replaced
        with placeholders in every screen on this page.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The site</p>
    <h2>Nine pages, one question each</h2>
    <p>
      Every page answers one reader’s question, and no answer is more than a click from the top
      bar or the footer.
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-flow">
      {
        pages.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <strong>{p.name}</strong>
              <span>{p.text}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Members and contractors</p>
    <h2>Each side of the hall gets its own page</h2>
    <p>
      The members page explains the app in terms of what a member looks up: book position, dispatch
      ticket, card wallet, hours, dues, and coverage. Beside the copy is the member app itself, the
      actual prototype screen running inside a phone frame rather than a picture of one, so the site
      and the app can’t drift apart.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={membersApp}
        alt="The members page: six things the member app shows, the app store buttons, and the member app’s home screen in a phone frame"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The members page, with the member app’s live home screen beside the copy.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The contractors page does the same for the contractor portal, then spells out where the
      contractor’s view of the shared record stops. A contractor sees the members referred to them
      and the cards those members hold, but never the referral lists, another contractor’s business,
      or what a member’s health coverage was used for.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={contractors}
        alt="The contractors page: eight things the contractor portal does, from requesting workers to certifying payroll"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The contractors page: requesting workers, tracking a request, compliance, and certified
        payroll in one place.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Health and welfare</p>
    <h2>Benefits explained in plain language</h2>
    <p>
      Union health coverage is earned through hours worked, and that is rarely explained well. The
      Health &amp; Welfare page walks through it in four steps: you work the hours, the contractor
      pays the trust, your hours build an eligibility bank, and a monthly run checks the bank
      against the plan minimum. It then covers other plans a member can connect, and what to do
      after an injury on the job.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={healthWelfare}
        alt="The Health and Welfare page: Hours in, coverage out, with four numbered steps from working the hours to the monthly eligibility run"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        How coverage is earned, in four steps, with no figures a visitor would have to sign in to
        see.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">On a phone</p>
    <h2>Built for the phone in a vest</h2>
    <p>
      Members are likely to open the site on a jobsite, so every page collapses to a single column below
      desktop width. The menu folds into a full-width panel with large tap targets, and it closes on
      a link tap, on Escape, or when the window grows back to desktop size. The home page carousel
      takes a thumb swipe.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <div class="cs-phones">
        {phoneScreens.map((s) => <Screenshot src={s.src} alt={s.alt} widths={[300, 500, 700]} sizes={phoneSizes} class="cs-screen cs-phone" />)}
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The home page, the mobile menu, and the Health &amp; Welfare steps on a phone.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Getting in touch</p>
    <h2>A person reads it, not a queue</h2>
    <p>
      The contact page leads with what most people actually need: the hall’s address, phone, office
      hours, and dispatch window. The written-enquiry form asks for four things, says exactly which
      ones are still missing, promises the details are used only to reply, and confirms with a
      reference number. The website has its own sign-in, which tells members up front that a code
      goes to the phone the hall has on file.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot cs-shot--wide">
      <div class="cs-pair">
        <Screenshot
          src={contact}
          alt="The contact page: cards for the hall address, phone, fax, office hours, dispatch window, and membership meeting, above the start of a contact form"
          widths={[600, 900, 1400]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
        <Screenshot
          src={signin}
          alt="The website sign-in page: a member number or email and password form beside a photo of two workers reviewing a clipboard"
          widths={[600, 900, 1400]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>The contact page and the website’s sign-in.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Engineering highlights</p>
    <h2>Small rules that keep the site honest</h2>
    <div class="cs-features">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="layers" /></span>
        <h3>The platform’s design system</h3>
        <p>
          The site uses the same treatment as the platform’s shared sign-in: the same typefaces,
          color tokens, icon set, and corner radii. One raised panel per page at most, and everything
          else is separated by a single hairline.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="compass" /></span>
        <h3>Navigation from one table</h3>
        <p>
          Each page builds its top bar and footer from a single page map, so a page can’t be called
          one thing in the menu and another in the footer. A page that hasn’t shipped yet shows as a
          disabled “not published yet” entry instead of a link to a 404.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="check-circle" /></span>
        <h3>Copy that can’t overclaim</h3>
        <p>
          A visitor who hasn’t signed in has no local to read numbers from, so the public pages carry
          none. A self-check on every page warns in the console if the copy ever states a rate, a
          percentage, or an hour total.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="target" /></span>
        <h3>A carousel that gets out of the way</h3>
        <p>
          The home page slider advances on its own, but pauses on hover, keyboard focus, a swipe, or
          when the tab is in the background. Arrow keys and dots pick a slide, screen readers hear it
          as a carousel, and reduced-motion settings turn the movement off.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="users" /></span>
        <h3>Accessible by default</h3>
        <p>
          Visible focus rings, a skip link past the navigation, labeled form fields with the right
          autocomplete hints, decorative icons hidden from screen readers, and 44-pixel rows in the
          mobile menu.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="file-text" /></span>
        <h3>Self-contained pages</h3>
        <p>
          Like the platform’s prototype, every page is one HTML file with no build step, so any page
          can be opened, reviewed with union staff, or sent on its own.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={homeSurfaces}
        alt="The home page carousel on its Contractor Portal slide, with a photo of a worker holding a floor plan, above the About Us band"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The home page carousel: one slide for each part of the platform, with the photo changing
        alongside the copy.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">What’s next</p>
    <h2>From prototype to launch</h2>
    <p>
      In the production build the site moves to Next.js on AWS Amplify, alongside the union
      operations and contractor portals. Its sign-in gets wired to the platform’s Cognito sign-in so
      it hands members straight into their accounts, and the legal pages (privacy, HIPAA, terms, and
      accessibility) go live once counsel supplies the text.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
